    <h1>AUSSIE PRIMARY ACADEMY · PARENT & CHILD LEARNING GUIDE</h1>
    <h2>Angles for Years 3–5: A Step-by-Step Guide for Parents and Kids</h2>
    
    <p>Your child points to two angles and says, “That one is bigger because its lines are longer.”</p>
    <p>It is an understandable mistake. Long lines take up more space on the page. But an angle measures the amount of turn between two arms—not how long those arms are.</p>
    <p>Before reaching for a protractor, start with something familiar: the corner of a sheet of paper. That simple right angle gives children a useful reference for comparing the angles they see in books, doorways and shapes.</p>
    <p>This guide explains what angles are, how to recognise the main types, and how to practise at home or in the classroom.</p>

    <h3>Choose your starting point</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Start with the foundations</li>
        <li>Year 3: Compare with a right angle</li>
        <li>Year 4: Explore angle names</li>
        <li>Year 5: Begin measuring</li>
        <li>Try the practice questions</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Our learning approach</h3>
    <p>Understand → See → Try → Explain → Practise → Check</p>
    <p>Use this guide in short sessions. Choose one idea, explore its picture and ask your child to explain their thinking before moving on.</p>

    <h1>What is an angle?</h1>
    <p>An angle is formed by two rays that start at the same point. We often call these rays the arms of the angle. Their shared starting point is the vertex.</p>
    <p>An angle tells us how far one arm would turn around the vertex to reach the other arm in the direction shown.</p>
    <p>Try this with your arms: hold one arm pointing forwards, then turn your other arm out to the side. The turn between them represents an angle.</p>

    <p>Three words help children describe a diagram:</p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Word</th>
                <th>What it means</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Arms</strong></td>
                <td>The two rays that form the angle</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Vertex</strong></td>
                <td>The point where the arms meet</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Degrees (°)</strong></td>
                <td>A unit used to measure the size of a turn</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p><strong>Remember:</strong> Making the drawn arms longer does not change the angle. Changing the amount of turn does.</p>

    <h1>Start with a right angle</h1>
    <p>A right angle measures 90°. It is the same amount of turn as a quarter turn.</p>
    <p>The square corner of a sheet of paper is a useful right-angle checker. Place its corner on the vertex of an angle and line up one paper edge with one arm. Then compare the other arm with the other paper edge.</p>
    
    <ul>
        <li>If both arms line up with the paper edges, the angle is a right angle.</li>
        <li>If the marked opening is smaller, it is less than a right angle.</li>
        <li>If the marked opening is larger, it is greater than a right angle.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Look for right angles at the corners of a rectangular book, a square tile or a rectangular whiteboard. Ask children to check rather than guess from appearance alone.</p>
    <p><strong>Try saying:</strong> “I know this is a right angle because it matches the square corner of my paper.”</p>

    <h1>Types of angles: a simple reference</h1>
    <p>Once children can compare angles with a right angle, introduce the names below. The degree measures are a reference; younger learners can begin with comparisons without measuring every angle.</p>

    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Angle type</th>
                <th>Size</th>
                <th>Example</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Acute</strong></td>
                <td>Greater than 0° and less than 90°</td>
                <td>40°</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Right</strong></td>
                <td>Exactly 90°</td>
                <td>A quarter turn</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Obtuse</strong></td>
                <td>Greater than 90° and less than 180°</td>
                <td>120°</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Straight</strong></td>
                <td>Exactly 180°</td>
                <td>A half turn</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Reflex</strong></td>
                <td>Greater than 180° and less than 360°</td>
                <td>240°</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Full turn, or revolution</strong></td>
                <td>Exactly 360°</td>
                <td>One complete turn</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p><em>Follow the purple arc: it marks the angle being described. Diagrams show the stated turns.</em></p>

    <h2>Acute angles: smaller than a right angle</h2>
    <p>An acute angle is smaller than 90°, but greater than 0°.</p>
    <p>Imagine a door opened only a little. Viewed from above, the smaller angle between the door and its closed position could be acute.</p>
    <p><strong>Worked example:</strong> An angle measures 35°. Since 35° is greater than 0° and less than 90°, it is acute.</p>

    <h2>Obtuse angles: between a right angle and a straight angle</h2>
    <p>An obtuse angle is greater than 90° but less than 180°.</p>
    <p>The smaller angle between the hands of an analogue clock at exactly 4 o’clock is 120°, so it is obtuse.</p>
    <p><strong>Worked example:</strong> An angle measures 115°. It is larger than a right angle and smaller than a straight angle, so it is obtuse.</p>

    <h2>Straight, reflex and full-turn angles</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>A straight angle is a half turn: 180°. Its arms point in opposite directions along a straight line.</li>
        <li>A reflex angle goes beyond a half turn but stops before a full turn. For example, 270° is a reflex angle and represents a three-quarter turn.</li>
        <li>A full turn is 360°. You finish facing the direction you started from.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Check the marked arc:</strong> Two arms can show a smaller angle and a larger angle around the outside. The curved angle mark tells you which turn to describe. Do not assume every question asks for the smaller angle.</p>

    <h1>How to name an angle using letters</h1>
    <p>You may see an angle written as ∠AOB.</p>
    <p>The middle letter, O, names the vertex. One arm passes through A and the other passes through B.</p>
    <p>For a simple diagram showing one marked angle, ∠AOB and ∠BOA name that same angle.</p>
    <p><strong>Parent or teacher tip:</strong> Ask, “Which letter tells us where the arms meet?” before asking children to name several angles independently.</p>

    <h1>Three common angle mistakes—and how to fix them</h1>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>“Longer arms mean a bigger angle.”</strong><br>
            Draw two 45° angles, one with short arms and one with long arms. Both have the same amount of turn.<br>
            <em>Ask:</em> “What changed—the length of the lines or the amount of turn?” Both angles are 45°. Longer arms do not make a larger turn.</li>
        <li><strong>“A tilted right angle is no longer a right angle.”</strong><br>
            Rotate a rectangular book on the table. Its corners remain 90° even when they no longer look like an upright L.<br>
            <em>Ask:</em> “Does our paper corner still fit?”</li>
        <li><strong>“Every angle bigger than 90° is obtuse.”</strong><br>
            An obtuse angle must also be smaller than 180°. An angle of 220° is reflex.<br>
            <em>Ask:</em> “Is it between a quarter turn and a half turn, or has it gone past a half turn?”</li>
    </ol>

    <h1>Three easy angle activities</h1>
    <h3>Activity 1: Go on a right-angle hunt</h3>
    <p><strong>You need:</strong> A sheet of paper with a square corner.<br>
    Find five corners around the room. Before checking each one, predict whether it is smaller than, equal to or greater than a right angle.<br>
    Record the object, your prediction and what the checker shows.<br>
    <em>Thinking question:</em> “What evidence supports your answer?”</p>

    <h3>Activity 2: Make angles with two paper strips</h3>
    <p><strong>You need:</strong> Two paper strips placed with one end overlapping.<br>
    Hold the overlapping ends still to represent the vertex. Move one strip to change the angle.<br>
    Make an acute angle, a right angle and an obtuse angle. Then rotate the whole model without changing the opening.<br>
    <em>Thinking question:</em> “Did rotating the model change the angle type? Explain.”</p>

    <h3>Activity 3: Make turns with your body</h3>
    <p>Stand facing a fixed object. Make a quarter turn, half turn and full turn. Older children can also try a three-quarter turn.<br>
    Connect each movement to its measure: 90°, 180°, 360° and 270° respectively.<br>
    <em>Thinking question:</em> “Why do two quarter turns make a half turn?”</p>

    <h1>When should children use a protractor?</h1>
    <p>In Australian Curriculum v9.0, the progression moves from comparing turns and right angles in Year 3, to naming and comparing angle types in Year 4, then estimating, constructing and measuring angles in degrees in Year 5.</p>

    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Year</th>
                <th>Relevant focus</th>
                <th>Curriculum code</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Year 3</strong></td>
                <td>Recognise angles as turns and compare them with right angles in everyday settings</td>
                <td>AC9M3M05</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Year 4</strong></td>
                <td>Estimate and compare named angle types using a right angle as a reference</td>
                <td>AC9M4M04</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
