    </table>
    <p><em>These are national curriculum references. Check your school’s curriculum and teaching sequence when planning lessons.</em></p>

    <p><strong>For children ready to measure:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>Estimate the angle first. Is it acute, right or obtuse?</li>
        <li>Place the protractor’s centre mark on the vertex.</li>
        <li>Align its zero line with one arm.</li>
        <li>Follow the scale that begins at 0° on that arm.</li>
        <li>Read where the other arm crosses the scale, extending the drawn arm if necessary.</li>
        <li>Check whether the result fits the estimate.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>For example, if an angle looks acute but the reading is 140°, check whether you used the wrong scale.</p>

    <h1>Quick practice: explain your thinking</h1>
    <ol>
        <li>An angle measures 50°. What type is it?</li>
        <li>How many right angles make a half turn?</li>
        <li>An angle measures 135°. Is it acute or obtuse? Explain.</li>
        <li>You turn a square upside down. Have its corner angles changed?</li>
        <li>Two angles both measure 60°, but one has longer drawn arms. Which angle is larger?</li>
        <li><strong>Challenge:</strong> The smaller angle between two arms is 80°. What is the reflex angle around the other way?</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Answers</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Acute:</strong> 50° is between 0° and 90°.</li>
        <li><strong>Two:</strong> 90° + 90° = 180°.</li>
        <li><strong>Obtuse:</strong> 135° is greater than 90° and less than 180°.</li>
        <li><strong>No:</strong> Each corner remains a right angle. Rotation changes orientation, not angle size.</li>
        <li><strong>Neither:</strong> Both are 60°. Arm length does not change the amount of turn.</li>
        <li><strong>280°:</strong> A full turn is 360°, and 360° − 80° = 280°.</li>
    </ol>

    <h1>Frequently asked questions</h1>
    <p><strong>What is the easiest way to explain angles to a child?</strong><br>
    Begin with a turn. Use two paper strips to show how one arm turns around a shared point. Then use a square paper corner to compare the opening with a right angle.</p>

    <p><strong>Is 90° acute or obtuse?</strong><br>
    Neither. Exactly 90° is a right angle. Acute angles are smaller than 90°, while obtuse angles are larger than 90° and smaller than 180°.</p>

    <p><strong>Do children need to memorise every angle name first?</strong><br>
    Start by helping them recognise and compare turns. Introduce the names alongside examples, and ask children to explain how they know. A correct label is more useful when a child can support it with a comparison.</p>

    <h1>Keep practising with a clear next step</h1>
    <p>Ask your child to find one angle, name or describe it, and explain the evidence. If they are unsure, return to the paper corner and compare again.</p>
